# LEVEL 7 → 8 — SEARCHING FILE CONTENTS WITH grep


## LEVEL GOAL
Find the password stored in `data.txt` next to the word "millionth".

## DESCRIPTION
The file contains thousands of lines, making manual inspection impractical. This level introduces `grep` — one of the most heavily used commands in Linux system administration and security.

## APPROACH
I explored multiple methods during this level:

```bash
# Method 1 – Direct grep (most efficient)
grep "millionth" data.txt

# Method 2 – Piped from cat
cat data.txt | grep "millionth"

# Method 3 – strings pipeline
strings data.txt | grep "millionth"

# Method 4 – awk
awk '/millionth/' data.txt

# Method 5 – sed
sed -n '/millionth/p' data.txt
```

All methods returned the same result. The direct `grep` call was the cleanest and most efficient.

> Password obtained. [Not shared per OverTheWire rules]

## KEY TAKEAWAY
`grep` is the go-to tool for searching log files, configuration files, and command output. In real-world security work it is used constantly — for example, filtering an access log for a specific IP address or searching tool output for vulnerable entries.
